[{:seon.task/name "a-datom-costs-its-index-inserts"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by pure-tests, 2026-10-23 (main 1343c920, Datahike 7de92bb, Jolt e0351af8; ticks by jolt.host/ticks-of, allocation by seon.profile/alloc-bytes). Every bin/test and bin/prove plan transacts the import of src and test into a test-store branch: test-runner/program-db, d/db-with of seon.source/import-tx's 11,860 entity maps, 75,683 datoms, 5.4-6.3 s CPU and 3.6 GB, the largest phase of the cold plan (notes/pure-tests/plan-cold-phases.clj), linear, ~24.7k ticks and ~48 KB per datom. Layers per datom (notes/pure-tests/transact-cost-layers.clj): a transient sorted-set conj! 1.3k ticks; a :memory db, maps with tempids 13.2k ticks, 23 KB; explicit [:db/add e a v] 10.4k, 15 KB; a :db/unique identity x1.6; a content-id identity x2.8.
Cause, found 2026-10-23 (worktree session datom-cost), two producers:
- Jolt: a protocol call on Datahike's 21-field DB read every field on the method's entry, 158 ticks against 37 on a 2-field record, ~30 calls per datom (dbi/-config 18, dbi/-attrs-by 11); and update consed a rest list per call. Task jolt.passes.fold/a-method-reads-the-fields-it-names, Jolt 0d39edc3.
- Datahike (jolt/datom-cost 6db7188, pinned as 57f687f with jolt/release-drains merged in): the card-one check took the first of an :eavt slice, a seq costing 1.5k ticks and 5.4 KB at 2k datoms, as much as an insert: now di/-lookup with dd/cmp-datoms-ea, one descent; with-datom-upsert updated the temporal indexes for a first value, which leaves nothing there, copying the DB record each time; one-key update-in, additive-index-attribute resolving the attribute again for every datom, a fnil per datom and a concat per op in transact-tx-data.
Measured, per plain datom on the test store's seed (history kept), seon.db-test/a-datom-costs-its-index-inserts: 12.5k ticks and 18.2 KB before, 7.2k and 11.1 KB after (with jolt/release-drains merged in; 6.7k before it). The floor, measured on the same seed at 1,000: two current-index inserts 5.5k ticks and 7.0 KB, the card-one descent 0.95k and 1.4 KB, the datom 0.5 KB, two tx-data conjs 0.55 KB, five DB record copies 0.3k and 1.0 KB: ~6.8k and ~10.5 KB, so a plain datom now costs its floor. Maps with tempids on a :memory db: 13.2k ticks and 23 KB before, 7.3k and 18.6 KB after (entity-map->op-vec and tempid allocation, not yet traced). Identities are task an-identity-costs-its-seek."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-datom-costs-its-index-inserts}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-identity-costs-its-seek"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-23 (worktree session datom-cost), after a-datom-costs-its-index-inserts brought a plain datom to its floor (6.7k ticks, 11.1 KB on the test store's seed). An entity map named by an identity costs far past what its identity adds: per datom, half of them the identity, at 1,000 on the seed (seon.db-test/an-identity-costs-its-seek), :db/unique (:seon.task/id) 11.5k ticks and 34 KB, a content id (:form/id) 16.9k ticks and 65 KB (before Jolt record-field-binds and Datahike jolt/datom-cost: 20.7k and 43 KB, 27.2k and 73 KB). What the identity must add: its :avet insert (half a plain datom's two inserts), the unique check's :avet descent (validate-datom-upsert, ~1 KB), the entity's upsert seek (upsert-eid), and for a content id one hash of [attribute value] (content-tempids, content-eid: hasch's uuid): ~15 KB per datom, not 34-65. Not yet traced: where entity-map->op-vec, upsert-eid, the tempid bookkeeping and hasch spend their bytes. Measured on the way: a dd/datom is 481 B; tempid? and tx-id? allocate ~220 B each per op on a numeric e (Jolt's =, neg?); validate-val 560 B. Next: split the map path as the plain one was (time each part at n <= 100 by jolt.host/ticks-of and seon.profile/alloc-bytes), largest first, and fix at the producer, Datahike or Jolt."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"datahike.transaction/a-datom-costs-its-index-inserts"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/an-identity-costs-its-seek}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-keyword-attributes-two-values-are-two-values"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by form-keywords, 2026-10-25: datahike.query-test names exactly two qualified keywords, :ns/name and :query/nested-call, and test-runner/program-db failed writing them as its form's `:form/keywords` (Bad entity value, must be keyword?), as a vector and as a set. Cause: Datahike's maybe-wrap-multival (reference-code/datahike/src/datahike/db/transaction.cljc) reads any two-element collection whose first element is a :db.unique/identity attribute as a lookup ref, whatever the attribute's value type; a lookup ref means something only as a ref's value. Fix in the fork (jolt/multival-lookup-ref): that branch also requires the attribute be a ref (dbu/ref?) or a reverse ref. Replaces nothing in Seon; the alternative, `seon.program/form-tx` writing keywords as [:db/add] datoms, would be a second write path."
  :seon.task/proofs #{datahike.transaction-test/a-keyword-attributes-two-values-are-two-values seon.program-test/the-keywords-a-form-names-are-recorded}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-replaced-value-is-retracted-in-the-report"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by seon.ctx-test/ctx-is-the-record-after-every-transaction (2026-10-25): Datahike's transact-report (reference-code/datahike/src/datahike/db/transaction.cljc) replaces a held cardinality-one value in the index (with-datom-upsert) but puts only the new datom in the report's :tx-data, so seon.ctx/patch, reading a transaction's datoms, kept a turn moved to another run under its old run too. Upstream's behaviour as well (replikativ/datahike main bf1dfdfa4e); DataScript's transact-add retracts the old datom in its report. Fix in the fork (jolt/tx-data-retracts, 336de2ab): the report carries the retraction before the assertion. Every reader of :tx-data sees one more datom per replacement. Replaces nothing in Seon; the alternative, seon.ctx dropping an entity's old link itself, would fix the reader, not the producer."
  :seon.task/proofs #{datahike.transaction-test/a-replaced-value-is-retracted-in-the-report seon.ctx-test/ctx-is-the-record-after-every-transaction}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-index-insert-allocates-nothing-extra"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by root's perf pass, 2026-10-27 (perf-2026-10-27.md): d/db-with of the boot import (~125k datoms) costs 1,914M ticks and 4.88 GB against an estimated need of 350-600M ticks and 50-250 MB. Measured (worktree session an-index-insert-allocates-nothing-extra, seon.profile/measure!): keyword compare (cmp-attr-quick) is not it, 6 ticks and 0 B. One transient :eavt insert is 2.2k ticks and 2.86 KB, 13 comparisons a descent; per comparison, Jolt's == in combine-cmp 32 B (jolt-num-equiv, a rest-args procedure called through its var) and, when e and a match, Datahike's Jolt compare-value 240 B (a try entered, 176 B, and two letfn closures). Fixed at both producers: Jolt jolt/num-equiv-two-args (a 2-arity, 0 B; make numequivalloc) and Datahike jolt/compare-value-no-try (two values of one scalar kind compare with no try). What stays: every interop call on a deftype (.lookup, .cons, .root) walks the method arms with no site cache (task jolt.backend/a-deftype-method-call-takes-a-site-cache), and the import's larger owners are outside the insert: per entity a content id's hash 43 KB (hasch), an identity's upsert ~16 KB, an entity map's expansion ~10 KB (task an-identity-costs-its-seek)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-value-costs-its-index-inserts}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-async-writer-shuts-down-without-hanging"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the fork audit (2026-10-27, forks-health.md item 4) and again by the fork-followups session once a take from Datahike's throwable-promise worked on Jolt (jolt 02f621ec): datahike.test.background-gc-test, gc-test and merge-base-test hung at 0% CPU; gc-test's and merge-base-test's hang was Jolt's alts! taking from a non-channel port twice (fixed in jolt 02f621ec: both finish and are in the forks baseline), and background-gc-test and storage-retention-test (shared-holder-survives-and-draining-open-awaits-exit) still hang. Fiber dumps (kill -INFO; the logs a-gc-test.log, a-merge-base-test.log, a-background-gc-test.log in the root session's side-session-notes folder): two fibers, both parked on an alt-handler in jolt-fiber-<!*, one in datahike.writer/create-thread's commit loop under fail-pending-commits!, one in its processing loop under fail-pending-transactions! and writer-shut-down-error, after the connection's release closed transaction-queue. The writer's -shutdown joins both loops (<?- processing-thread, <?- commit-thread), so a loop parked forever holds the release. The async writer is not the one Seon uses (:sync), so Seon is not reached. Correct: a released connection's loops end, and both join the forks baseline (test/forks/baseline.edn)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{datahike.test.background-gc-test/background-gc-invokable-cross-platform datahike.test.storage-retention-test/shared-holder-survives-and-draining-open-awaits-exit}}]
